the exciting world of machine

00:00:19.013 --> 00:00:21.615
learning and run through a quick
demo of our self-paced lab.

00:00:21.615 --> 00:00:22.865
JOSH COGAN: Let's first recap.

00:00:28.737 --> 00:00:30.820
If you'd like to see a
deep explanation of machine

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.960
learning, you can check out
Yufeng's AI adventures episode

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:34.704
here.

00:00:34.704 --> 00:00:37.120
HEATHER CROSS: Machine learning,
artificial intelligence--

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.190
these are all major buzzwords
in today's cloud market.

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:41.310
But what's the difference?

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.684
JOSH COGAN: I like to think
of them as concentric circles

00:00:43.684 --> 00:00:46.660
where you have AI as the broader
concept, ML as the algorithms

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:48.550
to make AI work, and
then deep learning

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.161
is a type of ML using
multilayer neural networks.

00:00:51.161 --> 00:00:53.410
HEATHER CROSS: The core
activities in machine learning

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:56.920
include data gathering, model
building, training, evaluation,

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:58.414
and parameter tuning.

00:00:58.414 --> 00:01:00.580
JOSH COGAN: Gathered data
is used to train the model

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.230
and evaluate its performance.

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.480
Algorithms iteratively
learn from data,

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.850
and then can make
predictions on new examples.

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:08.980
HEATHER CROSS: Tagged people
inside photos, viewing

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.379
and buying suggestions,
personalized search results--

00:01:11.379 --> 00:01:13.420
machine learning is actually
behind a lot of what

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:15.086
we experience every day.

00:01:20.365 --> 00:01:22.990
JOSH COGAN: ML's a great way to
jump-start any application that

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:24.760
already has many
examples of what

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.480
you'd like the algorithm to do.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.060
Let's take a use
case from Google.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:30.610
Do you use the OK Google
functionality on your phone?

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:31.709
HEATHER CROSS: Every day.

00:01:31.709 --> 00:01:32.500
JOSH COGAN: Me too.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.041
Well, to build it,
they had to gather

00:01:34.041 --> 00:01:36.730
a bunch of different ways
people say, OK Google,

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:38.890
and use that to train
the ML algorithm.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.680
The research team collects and
annotates many other spoken

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.110
phrases so that the ML
can learn to process

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:45.412
different interactions.

00:01:45.412 --> 00:01:47.620
HEATHER CROSS: For those of
us without training data,

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:50.890
the Google APIs for vision,
natural language, translate,

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:53.170
and video intelligence
are already available,

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:54.878
and they're a great
place to get started.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:00.760
Now the fun part--

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:01.810
Qwiklabs.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.446
You can check out the link
to start the Qwiklab here.

00:02:04.446 --> 00:02:06.820
JOSH COGAN: This lab provides
an introductory walkthrough

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:09.910
on training a TensorFlow model
both locally and on Cloud

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:11.530
Machine Learning Engine.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:14.980
Next, we'll learn how to deploy
to Google Cloud for prediction.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:18.980
Keep in mind that this lab will
take about an hour to complete.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:20.990
Here, we've copied
in some training data

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:24.480
from the census data set,
validated the model locally,

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.580
and run a single instance
trainer using Cloud ML Engine.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.300
Now we're ready to deploy
the model to cloud.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.400
On CMLE, your model will be able
to make predictions and scale

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.980
to serve many
concurrent requests.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.800
Create a Cloud ML Engine model.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.410
Select the exported
model to use.

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:45.740
Set an environment variable
for the output path.

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:48.260
Deploy version one
of the trained model.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:50.330
This may take a few minutes.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:54.160
Send a prediction request
to the deployed model.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.200
The response includes
the predicted labels

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.872
of the example in the request.

00:02:57.872 --> 00:02:59.830
Here, we have the
probabilities that the income

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:02.980
less than or equal to
50,000 or that the income is

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.760
greater than 50,000.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.810
Well, that's all the
time we have for today.

